// @corpus: platform
// @id: PLATFORM-std-effects
// @category: platform — the host-independent effect vocabulary
// @charter: §7.5A, §10
// @milestone: E8
//
// The effects that exist wherever Pleris runs. Architect ruling, 2026-08-07:
// `pw-std` for `log` and `trace`, `pw-platform-web` for the rest — because a
// non-web host has no `dom.mutate` and should not inherit one.
//
// Both declare `capability none`, and that is an answer rather than a gap. An
// effect describes what computation DOES; a capability is the authority it
// needs; a host interface is one ABI representation of that authority. Writing
// to a log is an effect a deployment may observe and constrain, and it is not
// authority a host grants — an ontology that could not say so would force
// every effect to invent a capability it does not want.

module std.effects

// **This package exports its effect vocabulary.** Architect ruling,
// 2026-08-07:
//
// > Effect declarations are ambient only because the selected platform package
// > explicitly exports them into the Effect prelude. Their arguments are not
// > ambient.
//
// So a program using this platform writes `!{ database.read<Stores> }` without
// importing anything — but `Stores` still has to be in scope, resolved the way
// any other name is. Only the Effect namespace is exported; Term, Type, Ui and
// Event stay exactly as strict as assumption A-009 made them.
prelude Effect

// The privacy level is the point, and it is a type argument rather than part
// of the name: `log<Public>` names a sink whose contents anyone may read, so a
// `Secret<Payments>` reaching it (corpus R-006) is a flow the compiler sees.
// `packages/pw-platform-web/log.pw` declares the functions; this declares what
// they perform.
effect log<L> {
    capability none
}

// Diagnostic output for a developer, with no privacy parameter because it is
// not a sink application data reaches. Kept distinct from `log` so a
// deployment can drop one and keep the other.
effect trace {
    capability none
}
